  "summary": "One of the oldest pumps: a large screw — helical flights welded around a central tube — is set at an angle in a close-fitting trough with its lower end in the inlet basin. As the screw turns slowly, each flight scoops water and carries it upward in a pocket bounded by the flights and the trough until it spills into the outlet channel. Flow adjusts itself to the inlet level, efficiency stays high over a wide range, and large solids and fish can pass, so screws are used for wastewater lift stations, storm water, land drainage and return sludge.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Positive displacement (open screw) / kinetic lift",
    "configuration": "Inclined open screw in a trough, 3 flights"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "inclination_deg": 30,
    "flights": 3,
    "outer_diameter_m": 1.0,
    "lead_m": 1.3,
    "speed_note": "Slow speeds (tens of rpm, decreasing with diameter) — high-ratio gear reducer required."
  },
